éach term in Bayes' théorem has a conventional name.
The term P(A) is called the &lt;a href="prior%20probability"&gt;prior probability&lt;/a&gt; of A.
It is "prior" in the sense that it precedes any information about B.
P(A) is also the &lt;a href="marginal%20probability"&gt;marginal probability&lt;/a&gt; of A.
The term P(A|B) is called the &lt;a href="posterior%20probability"&gt;posterior probability&lt;/a&gt; of A, given B. It is "posterior" in the sense that it is derived from or entailed by the specified value of B.
The term P(B|A), for a specific value of B, is called the &lt;a href="likelihood%20function"&gt;likelihood function&lt;/a&gt; for A given B and can also be written as L(A|B).
The term P(B) is the prior or marginal probability of B, and acts as the &lt;a href="normalizing%20constant"&gt;normalizing constant&lt;/a&gt;.
